Architecture plan for ZeroClaw camera capture app

Goal
- Build a static, browser-only camera app that can start/stop/switch camera streams, capture photos, record short videos, persist media metadata and blobs locally, and render a responsive gallery.
- Keep the app deployable as static files with a simple local dev server and test coverage across storage, camera, capture, recording, UI wiring, and one Playwright smoke test.

Module structure
- package.json: npm scripts for dev, lint, format, unit tests, e2e smoke, and build verification.
- index.html: static HTML shell with app controls, media preview, status banner, gallery root, and dialog containers.
- src/main.js: application bootstrap, event wiring, state coordination, and rendering entry point.
- src/styles.css: responsive layout, camera controls, gallery, banner, empty state, recording indicator, and accessibility polish.
- src/storage.js: local persistence CRUD, schema normalization, quota rollback, and storage:changed events.
- src/time.js: unique ID and timestamp helpers shared by storage and tests.
- src/camera.js: startCamera, stopCamera, camera switching, feature detection, and friendly MediaDevices error mapping.
- src/capture.js: capturePicture(videoEl) canvas/blob helper.
- src/recorder.js: recordVideo(stream, options), recording guard, max-duration timer, countdown events/state.
- src/gallery.js: render persisted items and expose per-item download/delete/clear-all UI helpers.
- src/status.js: status/error banner helpers and user-facing message constants.
- test/*.test.js: Vitest unit tests using happy-dom and mocked browser APIs.
- test/setup.js: shared mocks for URL, Blob, canvas, MediaDevices, MediaRecorder, and storage events.
- e2e/smoke.spec.js: Playwright app-load/control smoke test with camera APIs mocked.
- .env.example: local configuration placeholders such as dev server port and optional deploy target notes.

Shared data shape
- Media item:
  - id: string generated by createId()
  - kind: "photo" | "video"
  - mimeType: string
  - createdAt: ISO timestamp from nowIso()
  - size: number
  - dataUrl: string for stored media payload
  - name: string deterministic download filename
  - durationSeconds?: number for videos
- Storage API should return normalized arrays and never expose mutable internal references.

Issue dependency map
- Issues 1-5 establish project layout, tooling, tests, local server, README, and deployment notes. These should be completed before feature implementation.
- Issues 6-10 build and verify storage. Issues 7-9 are internal dependencies for complete storage behavior before issue 10 tests are finalized.
- Issues 11-15 build and verify camera control. Issues 12-14 depend on start/stop basics from issue 11.
- Issues 16-18 build photo capture. Issue 17 depends on storage from issues 6-10 and capture from issue 16.
- Issues 19-22 build video recording. Issues 20-21 depend on the recorder primitive from issue 19.
- Issues 23-28 assemble the UI. Gallery work depends on storage; camera buttons depend on camera module; banners and empty states should be shared through status helpers.
- Issues 29-32 are integration, manual verification, accessibility/polish, and production/deploy hardening. They depend on the feature set being functionally complete.

Key design decisions
- Use plain JavaScript modules, HTML, and CSS with Vite/Vitest rather than a framework. The app scope is small, static, and browser API heavy, so framework state management would add little value.
- Store media as data URLs in localStorage for portability and simple tests. Keep schema small and implement quota rollback explicitly so failed writes do not corrupt existing data.
- Use CustomEvent on window for app-level events: storage:changed, storage:error, recorder:tick, recorder:state, and camera:error where useful.
- Keep browser API wrappers narrow and mockable. Camera, capture, recorder, and storage modules should be mostly independent so each issue can be tested in isolation.
- Prefer feature detection and friendly messages over throwing raw DOMException names into the UI.
- Treat HTTPS/secure-context requirements as first-class behavior in camera detection, README, and manual checklist.
- The app should remain usable without build output checked in; build verification should confirm the static bundle can be produced and served.
